WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.543
Parsing and validation should be deliberate steps.

2
00:00:02.543 --> 00:00:06.176
Parsing turns raw text or bytes into a structured value.

3
00:00:06.176 --> 00:00:10.536
Validation decides whether that value is acceptable for the program's contract.

4
00:00:10.536 --> 00:00:16.713
In C++, those steps are often close together, but it is useful to keep the responsibilities distinct.

5
00:00:16.713 --> 00:00:22.163
Every parse result should report success or failure in a way the caller must handle.

6
00:00:22.163 --> 00:00:29.792
A clear result object, std::optional, expected-style return type, status code, or exception policy can all work when used consistently.

7
00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:34.516
The important point is that failure is visible and cannot be accidentally ignored.

8
00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:36.332
Partial parses deserve special attention.

9
00:00:36.332 --> 00:00:44.325
A conversion that reads a leading number and leaves trailing characters can make the program accept something other than the intended value.

10
00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:51.955
A parser should report how much input was consumed or otherwise reject extra content that does not belong to the contract.

11
00:00:51.955 --> 00:00:53.408
Avoid silent fallback behavior.

12
00:00:53.408 --> 00:01:02.491
Defaulting a failed parse to zero, an empty string, the first enum value, or a permissive mode can hide invalid input and move uncertainty deeper

13
00:01:02.491 --> 00:01:03.581
into the program.

14
00:01:03.581 --> 00:01:09.031
Defaults should be intentional and limited to cases where the contract says absence is allowed.

15
00:01:09.031 --> 00:01:12.301
Keep raw input separate from parsed and trusted values.

16
00:01:12.301 --> 00:01:15.571
Use names and types that make the distinction obvious.

17
00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:18.478
For example, raw_count_text and validated_count communicate different states.

18
00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:23.927
That discipline helps reviewers find places where a raw value might be used too early.

19
00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:25.744
Finally, make parse failures testable.

20
00:01:25.744 --> 00:01:31.921
Unit tests should cover invalid text, trailing content, missing fields, wrong delimiters, duplicate values, and boundary cases.

21
00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:37.734
A validation system is stronger when failure cases are ordinary, documented behavior rather than rare surprises.

22
00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:41.367
Parsing code should also avoid mixing diagnostics with acceptance decisions.

23
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:47.180
A clear error can explain what failed, while the caller still receives an unmistakable rejected result.

24
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.360
That separation keeps recovery paths predictable.
